# Transactions and isolation levels

ACID: atomicity, consistency, isolation, durability.

Anomalies:
- dirty read: see uncommitted data;
- non-repeatable read: read the same row twice, get different values;
- phantom: re-run a range query, new rows appear;
- lost update: two read-modify-write cycles overwrite each other;
- write skew: two transactions read overlapping data and write disjoint rows, jointly breaking a constraint (the on-call doctors example).

| Level | Dirty | Non-repeatable | Phantom |
|---|---|---|---|
| Read uncommitted | possible | possible | possible |
| Read committed | no | possible | possible |
| Repeatable read | no | no | possible (standard) |
| Serializable | no | no | no |

In PostgreSQL: read uncommitted behaves as read committed; repeatable read is snapshot isolation and already prevents phantoms but not write skew; serializable is SSI, which aborts transactions that could form a dangerous cycle. Retry on serialization failure (SQLSTATE 40001).

Default is read committed. For the quota counters in our project we use `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` in a short transaction.
